WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.660
A dragon opens its mouth... What comes out? Fire? Smoke?
Or... bubbles?!

2
00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:16.938
Same prompt. Same model. Three controls can change what
gets picked. Let's turn them and watch!

3
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.735
This is a creative teaching simulation with invented
scores.

4
00:00:22.735 --> 00:00:26.745
It does not reveal any provider's private architecture.

5
00:00:26.745 --> 00:00:30.453
Available settings and their order can vary.

6
00:00:31.942 --> 00:00:38.072
Our prompt is: The dragon breathed... We'll imagine six
possible next tokens.

7
00:00:38.072 --> 00:00:41.633
Real models have much larger vocabularies.

8
00:00:43.108 --> 00:00:48.098
The model gives each token a raw score, called a logit.

9
00:00:48.098 --> 00:00:55.457
Softmax turns those scores into probabilities that add
up to one hundred percent.

10
00:00:56.942 --> 00:01:03.002
Fire starts at about forty one percent. Bubbles has
about nine percent.

11
00:01:03.002 --> 00:01:07.958
These are invented chances of selection, not truth
scores.

12
00:01:09.442 --> 00:01:13.862
First up: temperature! Keep every token eligible.

13
00:01:13.862 --> 00:01:20.866
Predict this: if we lower temperature, will the leading
bar grow or shrink?

14
00:01:22.817 --> 00:01:30.166
Let's find out. Slide temperature from one to zero point
five. Watch fire's probability climb!

15
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:39.020
Why? Every logit is divided by temperature. Two divided
by zero point five becomes four.

16
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.997
One point five becomes three. The score gap doubles.

17
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:51.188
There it goes! Fire rises from forty one to sixty three
percent.

18
00:01:51.188 --> 00:01:56.943
Its lottery slice grows, but other tokens can still win.

19
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.905
Now turn temperature up to two. Whoa, look at the
smaller bars!

20
00:02:03.905 --> 00:02:08.977
They gain probability as the distribution spreads out.

21
00:02:10.442 --> 00:02:16.492
Fire falls to about twenty eight percent. Bubbles rises
to thirteen percent.

22
00:02:16.492 --> 00:02:21.022
Higher temperature makes less likely choices more
competitive.

23
00:02:22.483 --> 00:02:29.043
Try the same illustrative random draw with both
settings. At low temperature: fire.

24
00:02:29.043 --> 00:02:33.502
At high temperature: bubbles! The scores never changed.

25
00:02:34.983 --> 00:02:43.478
At zero point two, fire has about ninety two percent.
The wheel is almost all fire. Predictable?

26
00:02:43.478 --> 00:02:46.496
Yes. Automatically correct? No.

27
00:02:47.983 --> 00:02:50.358
Temperature reshapes the chances.

28
00:02:50.358 --> 00:02:56.308
It does not reorder the original scores or change the
model's learned weights.

29
00:02:56.308 --> 00:03:00.690
Zero is commonly handled as greedy selection.

30
00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:08.485
Reset temperature to one. Now meet top k: the guest
list!

31
00:03:08.485 --> 00:03:13.161
You choose how many leading tokens get into the lottery.

32
00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:21.994
Set k to three. Fire, smoke, and sparks stay. Bubbles,
confetti, and ice are out!

33
00:03:23.483 --> 00:03:27.468
Those three originally total about eighty two percent.

34
00:03:27.468 --> 00:03:34.338
Rescale their chances to fill the whole wheel: about
fifty point six, thirty point seven,

35
00:03:34.338 --> 00:03:37.316
and eighteen point six percent.

36
00:03:38.775 --> 00:03:44.435
Rescaling just means dividing each remaining chance by
the total left.

37
00:03:44.435 --> 00:03:48.840
Fire's forty one percent becomes about fifty one
percent.

38
00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:55.026
We are sharing one whole pie again, not applying softmax
to probabilities.

39
00:03:56.483 --> 00:04:01.703
Now, a challenge. What happens at k equals one?

40
00:04:01.703 --> 00:04:04.568
Pause and guess...

41
00:04:04.568 --> 00:04:07.388
Exactly! Only fire remains.

42
00:04:07.388 --> 00:04:10.493
Its chance is one hundred percent.

43
00:04:12.483 --> 00:04:15.968
Raise k again. The other candidates return.

44
00:04:15.968 --> 00:04:21.957
K is configurable where supported; it is not secretly
chosen by temperature.

45
00:04:23.442 --> 00:04:26.602
Top k controls the candidate count.

46
00:04:26.602 --> 00:04:34.252
Which tokens make that list can change with the prompt
and with every new generation step.

47
00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:39.583
Keep temperature at one and disable top k.

48
00:04:39.583 --> 00:04:48.040
Top p asks a different question: how many leading tokens
reach our probability target?

49
00:04:49.525 --> 00:04:57.400
Set p to ninety percent. Start with fire: forty one
percent. Add smoke: about sixty seven.

50
00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.647
Still not enough!

51
00:05:01.108 --> 00:05:09.118
Add sparks: about eighty two percent. Add bubbles: about
ninety one. Ding! We crossed ninety.

52
00:05:09.118 --> 00:05:11.680
Keep these four and stop.

53
00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:19.115
Lower p to sixty five percent. Now fire and smoke are
enough!

54
00:05:19.115 --> 00:05:23.715
The same scores produce a smaller eligible set.

55
00:05:25.192 --> 00:05:31.047
Raise p to ninety five percent. Four are no longer
enough.

56
00:05:31.047 --> 00:05:37.087
Confetti joins the party, bringing the total to about
ninety seven percent.

57
00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:46.047
Here's the trick: p measures combined probability, not a
percentage of vocabulary tokens.

58
00:05:46.047 --> 00:05:50.923
We include whole tokens, so the total may overshoot the
target.

59
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:59.225
Hold p at ninety percent. At temperature one, four
tokens make the cut.

60
00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:02.972
Now lower temperature and watch the meter.

61
00:06:04.442 --> 00:06:12.637
At zero point five, just three tokens reach ninety
percent. At zero point two, fire alone does!

62
00:06:12.637 --> 00:06:16.802
P stayed fixed; temperature changed the distribution.

63
00:06:18.275 --> 00:06:22.005
Ready for the full experiment?

64
00:06:22.005 --> 00:06:29.774
Set temperature to zero point five, k to four, and p to
zero point eight five.

65
00:06:31.233 --> 00:06:37.563
First, temperature sharpens the probabilities. Then top
k keeps four candidates.

66
00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:43.589
In our demonstration, we normalize within that remaining
pool before applying top p.

67
00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:53.552
The first two now total about eighty eight percent,
which passes our eighty five percent target.

68
00:06:53.552 --> 00:06:56.526
Top p keeps fire and smoke.

69
00:06:57.983 --> 00:07:06.178
Normalize once more. Fire gets about seventy three
percent of the wheel; smoke gets twenty seven.

70
00:07:06.178 --> 00:07:09.886
Now the random picker chooses one token.

71
00:07:11.358 --> 00:07:17.208
And this illustrative draw lands on... smoke! A smaller
slice can win.

72
00:07:17.208 --> 00:07:21.481
Probability describes a chance, not a promise.

73
00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:29.742
Keep k and p fixed, but raise temperature to two. The
distribution flattens.

74
00:07:29.742 --> 00:07:33.951
This time, top p needs all four candidates!

75
00:07:35.442 --> 00:07:43.142
One more surprise: if k is one, changing temperature
cannot bring excluded tokens back.

76
00:07:43.142 --> 00:07:46.899
There is only one slice left to choose.

77
00:07:48.358 --> 00:07:56.428
Append the selected token to the prompt. New context
produces new scores, and the lottery repeats.

78
00:07:56.428 --> 00:08:00.709
These controls can shape the wording that follows.

79
00:08:02.192 --> 00:08:05.497
What about repeating words?

80
00:08:05.497 --> 00:08:11.762
Separate repetition penalties can lower the scores of
tokens already used.

81
00:08:11.762 --> 00:08:17.822
They encourage alternatives, but do not ban repetition
or guarantee a better answer.

82
00:08:19.275 --> 00:08:23.620
Quick challenge! Bigger or smaller slices?

83
00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:24.955
That's temperature.

84
00:08:24.955 --> 00:08:29.005
A fixed number of candidates?

85
00:08:29.005 --> 00:08:29.517
Top k.

86
00:08:29.517 --> 00:08:33.665
A cumulative probability target?

87
00:08:33.665 --> 00:08:35.469
Top p. You've got it!

88
00:08:36.942 --> 00:08:43.587
These are sampling controls, not truth controls. They
cannot guarantee fewer hallucinations.

89
00:08:43.587 --> 00:08:48.422
For factual answers, use reliable evidence and verify
important claims.

90
00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:56.865
Now you can picture it: temperature reshapes, k caps, p
filters, and sampling picks.

91
00:08:56.865 --> 00:09:01.362
Try one setting at a time. Keep experimenting!

